<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Styria</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Steiermark</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Styria (Steiermark) is a mountainous region of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a>. It takes its name from the castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steyr">Steyr</a>, seat of the Otakar margraves who ruled it from 1056. It became a duchy in 1180 and passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> in 1282, remaining one of their core lands. Its southern part, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-styria">Lower Styria</a>, went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> in 1919 and now belongs to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/styria.jpg" alt="Styria" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Made by Jon Harald Søby from File:Steiermark Landesfarben.PNG, colours and ratio adjusted by Mevsfotw. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Styria.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Styria, in German Steiermark, is geographically diverse. The northern and north-western parts, known as Upper Styria, are mountainous and include parts of the Northern Limestone Alps and the Central Eastern Alps, with valleys formed by the Mur, Mürz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enns-river">Enns</a> and Salza rivers. The central and southern parts include the Graz Basin, hill country, vineyards and thermal-spring regions. Its extensive forests earned it the name Green Heart of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and it is also called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/green-march">Green March</a> and, for its long heritage of manufacturing and engineering, the Iron Margraviate. The March took its name from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steyr">Steyr</a>, the seat of the Otakar dynasty, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-austria">Upper Austria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Celtic tribes inhabited the area. After the Roman conquest the east of what is now Styria belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> and the west to Noricum. Visigoths, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a>, Rugii and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> conquered or crossed it, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avar</a> domination settled the valleys around 600, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarians">Bavarians</a> under Frankish domination expanded southward and eastward and absorbed the Slavic population. Under the Otakar dynasty Styria became a margraviate in 1056 and in 1180 was separated from the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> to become a duchy of its own. Under the Georgenberg Pact the Austrian Duke Leopold V also became Duke of Styria in 1192.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-babenberg">Babenberg dynasty</a> ended, Styria came briefly under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, losing vast parts of its territory, including the former capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steyr">Steyr</a> and Pitten. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> reunified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1282 and Pitten returned to Styria until Austria finally annexed it in the 16th century. Styria formed the central part of Inner Austria with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>. Ottoman raids from the 15th to the 17th century caused severe devastation and depopulation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archduke-john-of-austria">Archduke John of Austria</a>, the so-called Styrian Prince, advanced its economy between 1809 and 1859.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the duchy was partitioned broadly along ethnic lines, the south, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maribor">Maribor</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celje">Celje</a> and Ptuj, passing to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>. This region, traditionally called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-styria">Lower Styria</a>, now belongs to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Styria is a state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in the south-east, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a> to the south. Its capital and largest city is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a>. It is one of Austria's major industrial and research regions, with mining, iron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> in Upper Styria and wine, fruit growing and spas in the south and east.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Styria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its lowest points came in 788, when Charlemagne deposed Tassilo III, and in the 10th to 12th centuries, when the kings repeatedly detached territory: Carinthia and the March of Verona were split off in 976, Austria in 1156 and <b>Styria</b>'s link was broken in 1180.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Ottokar defeated Béla IV of Hungary at the Battle of Kressenbrunn in 1260, securing <b>Styria</b>, and joined the crusades of the Teutonic Knights against the pagan Prussians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The victory gave Austria and <b>Styria</b> to the Habsburgs, who ruled Austria until 1918.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-26">26 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Count Rudolf IV was elected King of the Romans in 1273 as Rudolf I and in 1278 defeated Ottokar II of Bohemia, gaining Austria and <b>Styria</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-habsburg">County of Habsburg</a></li><li><blockquote>The Babenbergs acquired <b>Styria</b> in 1192 and made Vienna a centre of Minnesang.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-austria">Duchy of Austria</a></li><li><blockquote>It included Carantania and huge marches extending to Verona, <b>Styria</b> and Carniola, many of which later became separate.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-carinthia">Duchy of Carinthia</a></li></ul></section>
